    "explanation": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "title": "Initial Observation",
        "details": "Begin by observing the body at the scene. Look for indicators like livor mortis (postmortem lividity), which causes the blood to settle in the lowest parts of the body, creating a purplish discoloration. Note the position and extent of this discoloration as it typically becomes fixed 6-12 hours after death."
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "title": "Rigor Mortis",
        "details": "Check for rigor mortis, the stiffening of the muscles post-death. This process generally begins 2-4 hours after death, becomes fully established around 12 hours, and then dissipates after 24-48 hours. The presence and stage of rigor mortis can give clues about the time of death."
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "title": "Body Temperature",
        "details": "Measure the core body temperature. The body typically cools at a rate of 1.5 degrees Fahrenheit per hour until it reaches the ambient temperature. This is known as algor mortis. The rate can vary based on environmental conditions, clothing, and body size."
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "title": "Decomposition",
        "details": "Assess the level of decomposition, which includes observing changes like skin slippage, bloating, and insect activity. These can provide additional clues, especially in longer postmortem intervals."
      },
      {
        "step": 5,
        "title": "Gastric Contents",
        "details": "Examine the stomach contents. The presence and digestion level of food can help narrow down the time of death, as food typically takes 2-4 hours to leave the stomach."
      },
      {
        "step": 6,
        "title": "Forensic Entomology",
        "details": "Consult a forensic entomologist if insects are present. The life cycle stages of insects like blowflies, which are among the first to colonize a body, can provide a timeline for death."
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "By synthesizing these observations, a forensic pathologist can provide a more accurate estimation of the time of death, which is crucial for the investigation. Each method has its limitations, so multiple indicators are typically used to corroborate findings."
  }
}
